  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Humid with additional showers/thunderstorms \npossible into late week \n\nParade of convective complexes and associated cloud cover has \neffectively shut off precip across western PA for the time\nbeing. Farther to the east, widely scattered thunderstorm \ndevelopment is expected through the rest of the afternoon with\nan isolated threat for damaging wind gusts. More sunshine\nbeneath a cirrus canopy has driven temps into the mid 80s\nsupporting modestly steep low level lapse rates. Water loading\nhigher pwat air would also be supportive of localized\ndownbursts. This area will be monitored, but the probability of\na watch issuance is 5% per MCD #1928.\n\nConfidence in how the precip pattern evolves from west to east\nfrom the Ohio Valley later tonight remains quite low. This is \ndue in a large part to high variability in the CAM signals. We \nwill let the flood watch for Somerset County ride for now and \nallow evening shift to assess radar/model trends. Patchy fog is \nlikely to form late tonight into early Wednesday morning. \n\nUncertainty only grows with time through midweek. Slow moving\nfrontal boundary will continue risk of showers and heavy\nthunderstorms through Thursday; however tomorrow (8/12) appears\nto mark a nadir in both severe storm and flash flood risk. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Seasonably cooler and less humid Friday and\nSaturday \n\nComfortably cooler and noticeably drier (less humid) air \naccompanies seasonably strong/Canadian high pressure building \nsoutheast from the Upper Midwest Friday night through Saturday.  \nBelow normal pwats will facilitate pleasant days and crisp\nnights (40s and 50s most locations Friday night/AM Saturday.",
